import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Plan 0027 — the toy course, cycle 4 (delivery · pricing · approximation · payoff)","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"graphopt/project/plans/0027-toy-course-cycle-4.md","filePath":"vendored/graphopt/project/plans/0027-toy-course-cycle-4.md"}`),a={name:`graphopt/project/plans/0027-toy-course-cycle-4.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="plan-0027-—-the-toy-course-cycle-4-delivery-·-pricing-·-approximation-·-payoff" tabindex="-1">Plan 0027 — the toy course, cycle 4 (delivery · pricing · approximation · payoff) <a class="header-anchor" href="#plan-0027-—-the-toy-course-cycle-4-delivery-·-pricing-·-approximation-·-payoff" aria-label="Permalink to “Plan 0027 — the toy course, cycle 4 (delivery · pricing · approximation · payoff)”">​</a></h1><p>Depends on: plan 0026 (cycle 3 — minting/substrate/ports/honesty), plan 0012 (bounded-error rewrites), plan 0010 (performance), the <code>gen</code> kernel path (<code>catopt_discovery.genkernel</code>), ADR 0003 (four dimensions).</p><h2 id="motivation-—-measured-gaps-after-cycle-3" tabindex="-1">Motivation — measured gaps after cycle 3 <a class="header-anchor" href="#motivation-—-measured-gaps-after-cycle-3" aria-label="Permalink to “Motivation — measured gaps after cycle 3”">​</a></h2><p>Cycle 3 landed levels 12–18 (minting, truth-vs-pay, exposure, substrate, ports, honesty). Auditing the resulting 18-level ladder against the real system leaves four <strong>uncovered ideas</strong>, each central:</p><ol><li><strong>Approximation with certificates</strong> (plan 0012 / <code>error_budget</code>). The word &quot;bounded&quot; appears only in prose callouts. The ε axis — offering members, certifying a bound, and <em>refusing</em> when the economics do not work (the measured BiRefNet negative: 56 offers, 0 delivered, 1.32× worse than dense) — is a whole lesson with no level.</li><li><strong>Delivery</strong> (execution dimension). Level 16 swaps a backend; nothing teaches that a chosen program must <em>run fast</em> — executor tables, the fused carrier lowering (parity with the paper&#39;s own chunked implementation), CUDA-graph capture, and minted kernels (<code>genkernel</code> — the <code>chainfuse</code> 2.94× vs <code>torch.compile</code>).</li><li><strong>Pricing steers selection — and can be wrong.</strong> The measured executor-routing inversion (modeled −20…30 %, measured 1.9–2.7× slower; fixed by calibrated corrections) is the sharpest lesson about why wins are regime-dependent, and the course never states it.</li><li><strong>The payoff chapter.</strong> The stated objective is <em>understanding so the reader can improve the project</em> — but the ladder ends without mapping its ideas to the project&#39;s actual open work (plan 0025: enumeration ordering, grammar gaps, gate 3, the three law families) and their measured blockers.</li></ol><p>Plus one presentation gap: gamification is gating + persistence only — no visible progress meter, no completion recap.</p><h2 id="design" tabindex="-1">Design <a class="header-anchor" href="#design" aria-label="Permalink to “Design”">​</a></h2><h3 id="four-new-levels-19-→-22" tabindex="-1">Four new levels (19 → 22) <a class="header-anchor" href="#four-new-levels-19-→-22" aria-label="Permalink to “Four new levels (19 → 22)”">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>id</th><th>idea</th><th>interactive</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>19</td><td><code>bounded</code></td><td>approximation is allowed — with a certificate and an economics test</td><td>pick a budget; watch members offered → certified → <strong>delivered or refused</strong>; see the bound gate stay bitwise-clean while the <em>pricing</em> refuses; the BiRefNet numbers as the worked case</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td><code>deliver</code></td><td>the chosen program must run: same term, different deliveries</td><td>pick a delivery (generic / batched / fused / graph-captured / minted kernel) and see modeled cost vs the measured wall-clock story from the retros; the fused-lowering parity and <code>chainfuse</code> cases</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td><code>pricing</code></td><td>the cost model steers selection and can be wrong</td><td>live: a small route table where the model&#39;s pick ≠ the measured winner (the inversion); toggle calibration and watch the pick flip</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td><code>improve</code></td><td>the payoff: each idea → the project&#39;s open work</td><td>pick a named gap (from plan 0025), see what blocks it, what the next measurement is, and which level teaches the idea behind it</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="gamification-presentation-not-new-engine" tabindex="-1">Gamification (presentation, not new engine) <a class="header-anchor" href="#gamification-presentation-not-new-engine" aria-label="Permalink to “Gamification (presentation, not new engine)”">​</a></h3><ul><li>A <strong>progress meter</strong> in the shell (cleared / total), visible without scrolling.</li><li>A <strong>per-level cleared badge</strong> once its checkpoint passes.</li><li>A <strong>completion recap</strong> panel (after level 22): the ladder&#39;s one-liners in order — the course&#39;s own summary of itself.</li></ul><h3 id="engine-additions" tabindex="-1">Engine additions <a class="header-anchor" href="#engine-additions" aria-label="Permalink to “Engine additions”">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>pricing.ts</code> — a small route table with modeled vs measured costs, an uncalibrated and a calibrated model, and <code>pick()</code>/<code>best()</code> computing the inversion live.</li><li><code>delivery.ts</code> — delivery variants for one term with (illustrative, sourced) measured wall-clocks and a live modeled-cost column.</li><li><code>bounded.ts</code> — budget → offered/certified/delivered tallies with the economics rule (<code>bounded member must beat dense</code>) computed live over a small bank.</li><li><code>improve.ts</code> — the gap table (id, blocker, next measurement, level-that-teaches-it) as data.</li></ul><h3 id="constraints" tabindex="-1">Constraints <a class="header-anchor" href="#constraints" aria-label="Permalink to “Constraints”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Same house rules as cycles 1–3: one idea per level, live wherever computable, illustrative numbers labelled and sourced from <code>project/retros/</code>.</li><li>No overclaiming: the refusal paths are the point of levels 19/21.</li><li>Bump <code>STORAGE_KEY</code> to <code>v3</code>; <code>vp check</code> / <code>vp run -r test</code> / <code>vp run -r build</code> must pass; extend the engine + course tests.</li></ul><h2 id="falsification" tabindex="-1">Falsification <a class="header-anchor" href="#falsification" aria-label="Permalink to “Falsification”">​</a></h2><ul><li>If level 19 can be passed without understanding <em>why</em> a certified member is refused, it teaches the wrong thing — rewrite around the economics.</li><li>If level 21&#39;s inversion cannot be reproduced live in TS, it becomes a worked example in level 20, not its own level.</li><li>If level 22 duplicates plan 0025 verbatim, it is documentation — it must teach the <em>connection</em> (idea → blocker → next measurement), not restate the plan.</li></ul>`,17)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};